    "body": "ValidationState::Error() only sets the reject reason if the state is still valid. When it is called on a state that is already Invalid or Error, the mode changes but the earlier reason, result and debug message are kept. The result is a mix of the old and new error. Code review in #35570 found two places where this happens. This makes the in-and-out use of the state parameter harder to follow and to remove later.\n\nThis PR makes both cases explicit:\n\n1. `ConnectBlock()` → `CheckBlock()` with a mutated block (`BLOCK_MUTATED`, e.g. a merkle root mismatch). This is treated as a sign of corruption and reported through `FatalError()`, but the resulting Error state kept `BLOCK_MUTATED` / `bad-txnmrklroot`. Now the Invalid state is logged and then replaced, so the Error state carries the corruption message.\n\n2. `ActivateBestChainStep()` → `DisconnectTip()` → `FlushStateToDisk()` failure. `FlushStateToDisk()` has already reported a fatal error, and `ActivateBestChainStep()` then called `FatalError()` a second time with a less specific message. Now the existing error is logged and returned unchanged, and no second fatal error is reported.\n\nBehavior changes:\n\n- Case 1: the returned state has the reject reason \"Corrupt block found indicating potential hardware failure.\", no result and no debug message. The original reason is logged.\n- Case 2: the duplicate fatal-error notification is no longer sent. The returned reject reason does not change.\n\nThe first commit adds a unit test for case 1 that captures the current behavior, and the second commit updates its expectations. Case 2 has no test, because a flush failure cannot be triggered without fault injection. The path where `DisconnectTip()` fails while the state is still valid is covered by `feature_abortnode.py` and is unchanged.\n",
